{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>נזם זהב וחלי כתם מוכיח חכם וגו' (משלי כה, יב)</b>" "<b>King</b> Solomon, peace be upon him, admonishes in this verse (Mishlei 25) regarding the trait of reproof, for the entire Torah depends on reproof. For whoever accepts reproof and heeds the voice of his reprovers — lo, this is an exalted trait that encompasses all the goods, and it is the cause for the fulfillment of the Torah. And whoever hates reproof — a dispraised trait that encompasses the evils, and it is the cause for the nullification of the Torah, for he is close to heresy and to casting off from himself the yoke of Torah. And from this Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Mishlei 15): ״מוסר רע לעוזב ארח שונא תוכחת ימות״ — he explained that one who hates reproof is worse than one who abandons the path, for one who abandons a path — that is, one who casts off the yoke of a single commandment from upon himself — is punished with evil discipline (מוסר רע), which is suffering, but is not punished with the punishment of death. But one who hates reproof is not forgiven through evil discipline alone; rather, he is punished with the punishment of death — and this is what it says: ימות — for it does not suffice for him to be judged with evil discipline. Therefore Solomon admonished in this verse that a person should be one who accepts reproof, and he explained that reproof is the ornament (עדי) and glory of the soul, just as the nose-ring (נזם) and the necklace (חלי) are the ornament and glory of the body. And this is what it says: נזם זהב וחלי כתם מוכיח חכם על אזן שומעת — when a wise man reproves upon a listening ear, he likened that reproof to a nose-ring and a necklace, for the soul is adorned and beautified through the ornament of the nose-ring and the necklace. Reproof falls into two parts: one obligatory and the other prohibited. The obligatory is that we were commanded to reprove three classes, and they are: the wise, the simple, and the youth. The prohibited is that we were commanded not to reprove three classes at all, and they are: scoffers, fools, and the wicked. Scoffers — as it is written (Mishlei 9): ״אל תוכח לץ פן ישנאך״, and it is written (ibid.): ״יוסר לץ לוקח לו קלון״. And so we find in Yirmeyahu that he would discipline the people of his generation, who were scoffers, and they would curse him — that is what it says (Yirmeyahu 15): ״לא נשיתי ולא נשו בי כלה מקללוני״. Fools — as it is written (Mishlei 23): ״באזני כסיל אל תדבר כי יבוז לשכל מליך״ — for the scoffer will hate him, and the fool will despise his words and scorn the reason of his sayings. The wicked — as it is written: ״יוסר לץ לוקח לו קלון ומוכיח לרשע מומו״ — that is, one who reproves a wicked person and tells him his blemish also brings disgrace upon himself, for the wicked person will say to him, 'My garment is purer than yours'; or his blemish will revert back upon the reprove, for one who reproves a wicked person — lo, this itself is a blemish upon the reprove. These are the classes of the prohibited part. The classes of the obligatory part are the wise, as it is said (ibid. 9): ״הוכח לחכם ויאהבך״ — for sometimes the wise person needs reproof, when he sins occasionally inadvertently and by way of chance. The simple — as it is said (ibid. 1): ״לתת לפתאים ערמה״. Youths — as it is said: ״לנער דעת ומזמה״. And the essence of reproof is for the youth, because his instruments of intellect are not complete, and furthermore the evil impulse precedes within him the good impulse by 13 years, and all his thought and contemplation is only upon the pleasures of the world and the body. Therefore, while he is still a youth and his nature is soft, he needs reproof to serve as a bridle to guide him and bring him into the subjugation of the good impulse, so that he will heed counsel and the voice of his reprove and the words of the Torah, all of which is reproof — as it is said (ibid. 6): ״ודרך חיים תוכחות מוסר״. For if they leave him to habituate his nature in practice and conduct as he pleases, when he reaches the time of the perfection of his intellect it will be wearisome to reverse his conduct, and whatever conduct — whether good or bad — will never depart from him. And this is what is written (ibid. 22): ״חנוך לנער על פי דרכו גם כי יזקין לא יסור ממנה״ — for in the days of old age it is far-fetched to exchange his nature, with which he has already become habituated. Therefore it is fitting for him, while still a youth, to guard the words of reproof, and with this he acquires for his soul great benefit and great merit — and this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Tehilim 119): ״במה יזכה נער וגו'״. And because of this, Solomon always admonishes to reprove the youth with the rod of discipline — as it is said (Mishlei 22): ״אולת קשורה בלב נער שבט מוסר ירחיקנה ממנו״ — he says: even when the folly is bound in the youth's heart and tied there, having already become habituated to it for many days, do not despair and say there is no remedy for him, for since he is a youth, the rod of discipline will remove it from him. Reprove him little by little and reproof will enter his heart, for the speech of the reprove, when soft, together with persistence, will make an impression upon the heart of the reproved — just as you see with a drop of water, which is soft and yielding, yet with persistence bores a hole through hard stone. But if one despairs of him and does not exert himself in reproof, lo, this one hates him — and this is what Solomon, peace be upon him, said (ibid. 13): ״חושך שבטו שונא בנו ואוהבו שחרו מוסר״. And they said in the Midrash: חושך שבטו שונא בנו — this is David; ואוהבו שחרו מוסר — this is Yaakov. To teach you that whoever withholds discipline from his son, in the end he turns out to bad culture (תרבות רעה). For so we find with Yishmael, who had stirrings toward his father, and he did not discipline him, and he turned out to bad culture — as it is said (Bereishis 21): ״ותרא שרה את בן הגר המצרית אשר ילדה לאברהם מצחק״ — and מצחק is nothing but idolatry, as it is said (Shemos 32): ״ויקמו לצחק״. And so we find with Esav, who had stirrings toward his father, and he did not discipline him, and he turned out to bad culture and transgressed five transgressions on that day. And likewise Adoniyahu son of Chagit had stirrings toward his father, and he did not discipline him — as it is said (Melachim I 1): ״ולא עצבו אביו מימיו״. And likewise Avshalom — his father did not discipline him, and he turned out to bad culture, and lay with his wives, and caused him to be driven out of Yerushalayim, and to go up barefoot with his head covered, going up weeping, and he slew many tens of thousands of Israel. ואוהבו שחרו מוסר — this is Avraham, who disciplined Yitzchak and led him in his ways — as it is said (Bereishis 18): ״כי ידעתיו למען אשר יצוה וגו'״. And likewise Yaakov disciplined his sons, and there was no dross among them. And because Yaakov our father, peace be upon him, was a reproving sage, and the tribes had a listening ear, therefore they were like refined silver and were a golden nose-ring and a golden necklace, and they were the sons of their father. This is what is written:" "<b>ואלה שמות בני ישראל הבאים מצרימה את יעקב איש וביתו באו.</b> This book should have begun with אלה שמות without a ו, like the book אלה הדברים, but the letter ו comes to join the matter, for the matter is connected above in the section ויגש, where he mentioned ״ואלה שמות בני ישראל הבאים מצרימה יעקב ובניו״. And it comes to teach that it is one matter even though they are two books. He counted them there in their lifetime; he returns and counts them here after their death — this too is for their honor, and to make known their belovedness, as our Sages of blessed memory said: Israel are likened to the stars, which He brings out by number and brings in by number, as it is said (Yeshayahu 40): ״המוציא במספר צבאם לכלם בשם יקרא״. Therefore He mentions their names constantly and repeats them all in a way of love, just as we find the name Israel five times in a single verse." "<b>הבאים.</b> An expression of multiplication, from the language of (Yeshayahu 27): ״הבאים ישרש יעקב יציץ ופרח ישראל״. And since only seventy souls went down to Egypt, and the Holy One, blessed be He, decreed upon them the blessing — in the manner of what is written (Shemos 1): ״ובני ישראל פרו וישרצו וירבו ויעצמו במאד מאד״ — therefore He used of them the term הבאים, which testifies to the multiplication of their seed. <b>מצרימה.</b> This is the city itself, for wherever מצרים appears in Scripture the intent is the land of Egypt, but this verse testifies to this: (Bamidbar 20) ״וירדו אבותינו מצרימה ונשב במצרים ימים רבים״ — and it does not say ונשב שם, for the descent of the fathers was to the city itself, and the settlement of Israel was in the land of Egypt. And likewise HaShem said to Yaakov (Bereishis 46): ״אנכי ארד עמך מצרימה״ — that is the city itself, where the royal capital of the king was, but they did not settle there, only in its surroundings — as it is said: ״וישב ישראל בארץ מצרים״. <b>את יעקב.</b> 'With,' like את השמים ואת הארץ — by way of the Kabbalah. And likewise (Shemos 20): ״כי ששת ימים עשה ה' את השמים ואת הארץ״. <b>איש וביתו באו.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the verse comes to teach us that the tribes did not marry daughters of Egypt, for with their wives they came there. And by way of the Kabbalah: איש — after the manner of what is written (Shemos 15) ״ה' איש מלחמה״; וביתו — and His household, namely His court. And it is hinted here what our Rabbis of blessed memory said: they were exiled to Egypt, and the Shekhinah was with them." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> ״ואלה שמות בני ישראל הבאים מצרימה״ — but they came that day eighty years ago! Rather, all those eighty years while Yosef was alive they did not enslave them, and from the day Yosef died they placed a heavy yoke upon them, as it is said: ״וישימו עליו שרי מסים״. Therefore it is written הבאים — as though on that very day they arrived."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:41.582476777Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}